export const DV_COURSES = [{"id":"air-force-jrotc-1-2","title":"Air Force JROTC 1-2","dept":"JROTC (Aerospace Science)","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is designed to acquaint students with one of the following Aerospace Science academic blocks: Journey into Aviation History, Science of Flight, Space and Astronomy, and/or Air Force Survival Training. The Leadership Phase consists of drill, Air Force Customs and Courtesies, and health and wellness education. Students are required to wear the Air Force Junior ROTC Cadet uniform once a week. The uniform is provided free of charge.","cur":"jrotc"},
{"id":"air-force-jrotc-3-4","title":"Air Force JROTC 3-4","dept":"JROTC (Aerospace Science)","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"ROTC 1-2","desc":"This course is designed to acquaint students with one of the following Aerospace Science academic blocks: Journey into Aviation History, Science of Flight, Space and Astronomy, and/or Air Force Survival Training. Leadership education will alternate on a yearly basis between Communication Skill, Awareness, and Leadership and Life Skills and Career Opportunities. Uniform wear and drill is a weekly course requisite.","cur":"jrotc"},
{"id":"air-force-jrotc-5-6","title":"Air Force JROTC 5-6","dept":"JROTC (Aerospace Science)","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"ROTC 3-4","desc":"This course is designed to acquaint students with one of the following Aerospace Science academic blocks: Journey into Aviation History, Science of Flight, Space and Astronomy, and/or Air Force Survival Training. Leadership education will alternate on a yearly basis between Communication Skill, Awareness, and Leadership and Life Skills and Career Opportunities. Uniform wear and drill is a weekly course requisite.","cur":"jrotc"},
{"id":"air-force-jrotc-7-8","title":"Air Force JROTC 7-8","dept":"JROTC (Aerospace Science)","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"ROTC 5-6","desc":"This hands-on class allows the senior students the opportunity to put their theories of leadership and management to practice by leading the cadet corps. Students are responsible for planning, coordinating, communicating, and executing the corps' activities for the year. Students may also study different countries and cultures, and the government and military structure and policy-making procedures. Students will wear the uniform once a week to include the Battle Dress Uniform.","cur":"jrotc"},
{"id":"advanced-aerospace-science","title":"Advanced Aerospace Science","dept":"JROTC (Aerospace Science)","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"ROTC 3-4 & Instructor Approval","desc":"This program consists of an aviation ground school to prepare students to take the FAA ground flight exam. This step is a requirement for students interested in obtaining a private pilot’s license.","cur":"jrotc"},
{"id":"accounting","title":"Accounting","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Accounting","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is designed to help students understand basic accounting principles and procedures. Students will learn and apply the accounting concepts necessary for financial record keeping and decision-making in business using computers and real-world activities. Topics include careers in accounting, the importance of ethics, working with business transactions, completing the accounting cycle, and accounting for payroll, sales, and purchases. This course aligns with ACC 109 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: FBLA","cur":"accounting"},
{"id":"advanced-accounting","title":"Advanced Accounting","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Accounting","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Accounting","desc":"In this course, students will expand upon advanced accounting principles and procedures through business simulations covering accounting tasks and financial analysis. Students will use QuickBooks. This course is strongly recommended for students planning to major in business in college. This course aligns with ACC115 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: FBLA","cur":"accounting"},
{"id":"accounting-vita-certification","title":"Accounting – VITA Certification","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Accounting","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Accounting","desc":"The Accounting – VITA Certification course will provide students the tools and opportunities to certify through the IRS VITA program and real world experience preparing tax returns for people in our community who generally make $56,000 or less, persons with disabilities and limited English speaking taxpayers who need assistance in preparing their own tax returns. Students will explore the payroll process all the way from time card submission, to business tax calculations and individual tax return preparation. Student Organization: FBLA","cur":"accounting"},
{"id":"cte-internship-accounting","title":"CTE Internship – Accounting","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Accounting","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Accounting (Concurrently or previously taken)","desc":"A supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Accounting industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is delivered through the Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy. Student Organization: FBLA","cur":"accounting"},
{"id":"construction-technologies","title":"Construction Technologies","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Construction Technologies","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"The Construction Technologies program prepares individuals to apply technical knowledge and skills to residential and commercial building construction and remodeling. The Construction Technologies program includes instruction in construction equipment and safety, site preparation and layout, construction estimating, blueprint reading, building codes, framing, masonry, heating, ventilation, and air conditioning, electrical and mechanical systems, interior and exterior finishing, and plumbing. Student Organization: SkillsUSA","cur":"construction","check":true},
{"id":"advanced-construction-technologies","title":"Advanced Construction Technologies","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Construction Technologies","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Construction Technologies","desc":"Advanced Construction Technologies prepares individuals to industry aligned technical knowledge and skills in residential and commercial building construction and remodeling. Topics will include wall and floor framing, roof framing, exterior finishes, environmental systems, and electrical systems leading to tiny home construction of similar projects. Students will have the opportunity to learn NCCER certification, and the program will lead to apprenticeship opportunities.","cur":"construction","check":true},
{"id":"introduction-to-digital-communications","title":"Introduction to Digital Communications","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Digital Communications","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course covers the fundamentals of journalistic writing and desktop publishing. Emphasis is placed on interviewing, various styles of writing, publication design, and press law. Students will gain experience in digital communication and photojournalism. This course culminates in the submission of content for use in one issue of the school news publication. Student Organization: FBLA or SkillsUSA","cur":"digitalcomm"},
{"id":"advanced-digital-communications","title":"Advanced Digital Communications","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Digital Communications","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Journalism/Digital Communications","desc":"This course provides students with career-based experience in working on collaborative teams to prepare digital communications and journalistic products. Students in this class are responsible for production of the school’s student led newspaper / news publication. Students will apply practices learned in the introduction course as they prepare, layout, write and edit content for publication and examine use of social media. Digital and print products may be distributed within the school and community. Student Organization: FBLA or SkillsUSA","cur":"digitalcomm"},
{"id":"cte-internship-digital-communications","title":"CTE Internship – Digital Communications","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Digital Communications","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Intermediate Digital Communications or","desc":"This course is a supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the digital communications industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is delivered through Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy. Student Organization: FBLA or SkillsUSA","cur":"digitalcomm"},
{"id":"early-childhood-education","title":"Early Childhood Education","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Early Childhood Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course examines the developing child from conception through early childhood. Areas of study include the physical, social, emotional, and intellectual growth of children. Students gain an understanding of issues related to child development. Observation in a preschool setting provides students with real world experience. This course aligns with ECH176 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"earlychild"},
{"id":"advanced-early-childhood-education","title":"Advanced Early Childhood Education","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Early Childhood Education","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Child Development","desc":"Students will plan and teach curriculum for 3 and 4 year old children, design a learning center and gain valuable experiences working with children. The students will learn teaching techniques, childcare operations, CPR/first aid, and guidance skills. Credit for prior learning may be available from the community college for students who earn a passing score on the state’s technical skill assessment for Early Childhood Education. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"earlychild"},
{"id":"early-childhood-education-certificate","title":"Early Childhood Education Certificate","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Early Childhood Education","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Early Childhood Development","desc":"This course will provide an overview of early childhood education (birth to age eight) in American society, including current issues and responsibilities with an emphasis on issues of professionalism, ethics and program types. This course also provides an overview of the exceptional learner with emphasis on factors relating to current practices, identification, characteristics, and educational adaptations. Issues related to mild disabilities, serve disabilities, emotional and behavioral disorders, intellectual disabilities, and students who are gifted. Six (6 )credits can be completed concurrently at the college for CCL Completion and award. Includes field experience/Work based Learning. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"earlychild"},
{"id":"cte-internship-early-childhood-education","title":"CTE Internship – Early Childhood Education","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Early Childhood Education","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Early Childhood Education (Concurrently or previously taken)","desc":"This course is a supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Early Childhood industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is delivered through Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"earlychild"},
{"id":"education-professions","title":"Education Professions","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Education Professions","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course prepares students to work in the field of education. Units emphasize communication, knowledge of the learner, the teacher and the school. Students practice these skills through on-going field experience at one or two local elementary schools. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"eduprof"},
{"id":"advanced-education-professions","title":"Advanced Education Professions","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Education Professions","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Education Professions","desc":"This course continues to prepare students in the field of education. Units emphasize communication, classroom responsibilities, educational issues, and professional development. Students complete field experience at one or two local schools. This course aligns with EDU221 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"eduprof"},
{"id":"cte-internship-education-professions","title":"CTE Internship – Education Professions","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Education Professions","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Education Professions (Concurrently or previously taken)","desc":"This course is a supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Education Profession. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"eduprof"},
{"id":"film-tv-production","title":"Film & TV Production","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Film and TV Production","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"Students will be introduced to broadcast news, film, social media, and television arenas. This hands-on course will work in a digital media computer lab to capture, create, and edit video. Students use script writing, lighting techniques, and sound editing to create and produce a variety of projects. This course aligns with CIS120DK & CIS140 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA","cur":"film"},
{"id":"intermediate-film-tv-production","title":"Intermediate Film & TV Production","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Film and TV Production","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Media Productions","desc":"This course provides students with real-world experience in multimedia and video broadcast production, including social media formats, while using industry software for editing and effects. Students are teamed together to form production crews and given authentic experience with multimedia computers, video cameras, visual editing equipment, microphones, audio mixers and lighting equipment. Production material may be broadcast within the school and district. This course aligns with CIS220DK & CIS120DL at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA","cur":"film"},
{"id":"advanced-film-tv-production","title":"Advanced Film & TV Production","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Film and TV Production","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Intermediate Media Productions","desc":"Students will utilize multimedia technology to design and execute specialized projects in collaboration with team members using critical thinking and a solution focus to achieve pre-determined outcomes. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA.","cur":"film"},
{"id":"cte-internship-film-and-tv-production","title":"CTE Internship – Film and TV Production","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Film and TV Production","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Intermediate Media Production (Concurrently or previously taken)","desc":"This course is a supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Audio/Visual industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is an Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy course. Student Organization: SkillsUSA","cur":"film"},
{"id":"graphic-design","title":"Graphic Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Graphic Design","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"In this course, students study the fundamentals of graphic design through hands-on experience with relevant technology to prepare them to consider pursuing careers in the creative and dynamic visual communication industry. The combination of visual art and technical concepts sharpens students' skills in critical thinking, logic, communication, organization, and problem solving. Students will acquire technical skills in the use of Adobe design software. This course is aligned to CIS120DB/120DF at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit. Community college tuition applies. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA.","cur":"graphic"},
{"id":"intermediate-graphic-design","title":"Intermediate Graphic Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Graphic Design","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Graphic Design","desc":"This project-based course, applies the graphic design/visual communication techniques learned in the introductory class. Students are challenged to apply these methods and concepts to create effective designs for clients’ communication needs. This program prepares students for careers as production artists, graphic and multimedia designers within a diverse industry. Students will further develop technical skills in the use of Adobe design software. This course aligns with CIS138DA at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA","cur":"graphic"},
{"id":"advanced-graphic-design","title":"Advanced Graphic Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Graphic Design","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Intermediate Graphic Design","desc":"In this course, students refine their technical design skills. Students will further develop their problem solving skills, understanding of design and explore roles within a design team. Designers work on everything from illustrations, tag lines, animated logos, digital imaging, website design, and advertising layouts for clients. Students will apply advanced technical skills in the use of Adobe design software. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA","cur":"graphic"},
{"id":"cte-internship-graphic-design","title":"CTE Internship – Graphic Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Graphic Design","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Intermediate Graphic Design (concurrently or previously taken)","desc":"This course is a supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Graphic Design industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is delivered through Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA","cur":"graphic"},
{"id":"interior-architectural-design","title":"Interior Architectural Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Interior Design & Merchandising","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"Students will focus on creating residential and commercial spaces by applying the elements and principles of design and architecture to layout planning and mechanical drafting of floor plans, elevations and renderings. Students will create professional presentations by creating color schemes, lighting plans, finish treatments, and making furniture selections for specific uses. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"interior"},
{"id":"advanced-interior-architectural-design","title":"Advanced Interior Architectural Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Interior Design & Merchandising","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Interior Architectural Design","desc":"Students will create complex projects enhancing their studies of design, elevations, and architectural details for clients. Students will focus on sustainable practices in a global environment through real world scenarios. Students will use industry standard computer aided drafting software to prepare visual presentations. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"interior"},
{"id":"cte-internship-interior-design-merchandising","title":"CTE Internship – Interior Design & Merchandising","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Interior Design & Merchandising","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Interior Architectural Design (Concurrently or previously taken)","desc":"A supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Design and Merchandising industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is delivered through Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy. Student Organization: FCCLA","cur":"interior"},
{"id":"marketing","title":"Marketing","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Marketing","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is designed to introduce students to the fundamentals of marketing, including selling, promotion, pricing, product planning, distribution, and marketing information management. The concepts and principles learned throughout the year will be combined in developing a marketing plan and will lay the foundation for pursuing careers in marketing, management, and entrepreneurship. This course is aligned to MKT271 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit. Student Organization: DECA or FBLA.","cur":"marketing"},
{"id":"advanced-marketing-and-entrepreneurship","title":"Advanced Marketing and Entrepreneurship","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Marketing","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Marketing","desc":"This class provides students the opportunity to apply marketing and management principles for businesses through application-based projects. Students will prepare for employment by preparing career portfolios and working in a campus student enterprise. PASSING THIS COURSE MEETS THE FREE ENTERPRISE/ ECONOMICS GRADUATION REQUIREMENT. This course may align to MGT253 and GBS151 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. Community college tuition applies. Student Organization: DECA.","cur":"marketing"},
{"id":"software-and-app-design","title":"Software and App Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Software and App Design","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course introduces programming concepts and procedures through the use of one or more programming languages. Students will work to solve problems using programming languages through the design, execution, and refinement of solutions to ensure appropriate results. This course may align to CIA 156, CIS133DA, or CIS166AA at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA","cur":"software"},
{"id":"software-and-app-design-ap-computer-science-principles","title":"Software and App Design: AP Computer Science Principles","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Software and App Design","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course introduces programming concepts and procedures through the use of one or more programming languages. Students will work to solve problems using programming languages through the design, execution, and refinement of solutions to ensure appropriate results. This course may align to CIS133DA or CIS166AA at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FBLA","ap":"csp"},
{"id":"advanced-software-and-app-design","title":"Advanced Software and App Design","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Software and App Design","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Software and App Design","desc":"This course introduces programming concepts and procedures through the use of one or more programming languages. Students will work to solve problems using programming languages through the design, execution, and refinement of solutions to ensure appropriate results. This course may align to CIS162AB at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. PASSING THIS COURSE MEETS THE 4th CREDIT MATH GRADUATION REQUIREMENT. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or FLBA","cur":"software"},
{"id":"advanced-software-and-app-design-ap-computer-science-a","title":"Advanced Software and App Design: AP Computer Science a","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Software and App Design","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Software and App Design","desc":"This course introduces programming concepts and procedures through the use of Java. Students will work to solve problems using programming languages through the design, execution, and refinement of solutions to ensure appropriate results. This course is aligned to CIS163AA at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. PASSING THIS COURSE MEETS THE 4th CREDIT MATH GRADUATION REQUIREMENT.","ap":"csa"},
{"id":"introduction-to-sports-medicine","title":"Introduction to Sports Medicine","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Sports Medicine","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"Students will be introduced to the technical services involved in planning, organizing, researching, directing and controlling functions and processes related to the provision of select healthcare services. Focus will be on sports medicine. Each student may be required to spend up to 20 hours under the supervision of a Certified Athletic Trainer in the training room, athletic practices and work a minimum of one athletic competition event each season. Minimum hours required vary by teacher and location. This course aligns with HES154 & HES271 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. THIS COURSE DOES NOT MEET THE LAB SCIENCE REQUIREMENT. Student Organization: HOSA","cur":"sportsmed"},
{"id":"advanced-sports-medicine","title":"Advanced Sports Medicine","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Sports Medicine","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Sports Medicine","desc":"This advanced course prepares students to apply academic and technical knowledge in the field of sports medicine, physical therapy and athletic training. Each student will be required to spend a minimum of 30 hours under the supervision of a Certified Athletic Trainer in the training room, athletic practices and work a minimum of one athletic competition event each season. This course aligns with HES275 at the community college and may be available for dual enrollment credit. THIS COURSE DOES NOT MEET THE LAB SCIENCE REQUIREMENT. Student Organization: HOSA","cur":"sportsmed"},
{"id":"nasm-certified-personal-trainer","title":"NASM Certified Personal Trainer","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Sports Medicine","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Sports Medicine","desc":"The NASM Certified Personal Trainer (CPT) program is built on a proven, evidence-based training model that prepares students for realworld situations. Practical and application-focused content is presented in an easy-to-understand format. Students will master complex scientific principles and be prepared to accept the challenge of the certification exam. Student Organization: HOSA","cur":"sportsmed"},
{"id":"physical-therapy-tech","title":"Physical Therapy Tech","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Sports Medicine","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"The Certified Physical Therapy Tech focuses on rehabilitation services skills and knowledge. By obtaining a certification, individuals demonstrate they possess the necessary skills and knowledge to secure entry-level positions in the physical therapy industry. Student Organization: HOSA","cur":"sportsmed"},
{"id":"cte-internship-sports-medicine","title":"CTE Internship – Sports Medicine","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Sports Medicine","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Sports Medicine (Concurrently or previously taken)","desc":"This course is a supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Sports Medicine industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is delivered through Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy a Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy course. Student Organization: HOSA","cur":"sportsmed"},
{"id":"stage-production","title":"Stage Production","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Stagecraft","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course will introduce students to the technical and mechanical aspects of the theatre. Students will draft their own designs and construct stage scenery, as well as learn to use the lighting and sound equipment for the stage. Ten hours per semester of outside class time is required for work experience on school productions. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or Thespians","cur":"dv:STAGE PRODUCTION","bank":"theatre"},
{"id":"advanced-stage-production","title":"Advanced Stage Production","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Stagecraft","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Stage Production","desc":"Students will apply advanced technical and mechanical skills in the planning, designing and implementation of stage production projects while gaining valuable industry experience. Ten hours per semester of outside class time is required for work experience on school productions. Hours requirement may vary by campus. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or Thespians","cur":"dv:STAGE PRODUCTION","bank":"theatre"},
{"id":"cte-internship-stagecraft","title":"CTE Internship – Stagecraft","dept":"Career & Technical Education","program":"Stagecraft","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Stage Production (Concurrently or previously taken) Career Training Programs for High School Students","desc":"This course is a supervised work experience of a minimum of 5 hours per week (paid or unpaid) in a position within the Stagecraft industry. This internship provides on-the-job experience and valuable industry networking. Students must provide their own transportation to their worksite. ADDITIONAL INTERNSHIP SITE REQUIREMENTS MAY BE REQUIRED FOR STUDENT PARTICIPATION. A STUDENT APPLICATION AND TRAVEL FORM IS REQUIRED. Internship is delivered through Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy a Aspire Deer Valley’s Online Academy course. Student Organization: SkillsUSA or Thespians","cur":"dv:STAGE PRODUCTION"},
{"id":"art-humanities","title":"Art Humanities","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course will explore the role of art in past and present societies. Students will gain a deeper understanding of how art has evolved, how it is produced, and how it is used. Art concepts will be analyzed as a reflection of diverse cultures and of various historical time periods. Periodic studio activities may also be explored.","cur":"dv:ART1","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"music-humanities","title":"Music Humanities","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course will explore current and past musical forms. Students will gain a deeper understanding of the evolution of music, how it is produced, and how it is used. Music will be analyzed to provide a better understanding of history and culture. This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites.","bank":"music"},
{"id":"art-1-2","title":"Art 1-2","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course teaches students the basic techniques of painting and drawing using multiple art media including, but not limited to, graphite, ink, colored pencils, watercolor, and multi-media approaches.","cur":"dv:ART1","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"art-3-4","title":"Art 3-4","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Art 1-2","desc":"This course teaches students the basic elements, principles, and techniques of painting and drawing using multiple media such as but not limited to graphite, ink, colored pencils, charcoal, pastels, acrylic, watercolors, and oils.","cur":"dv:ART2","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"art-5-6","title":"Art 5-6","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Art 3-4","desc":"This course offers more advanced elements, principles, and techniques of painting and drawing using multiple media such as but not limited to graphite, ink, colored pencils, charcoal, pastels, acrylic, watercolors, and oils. Color theory will be explored and an emphasis will be placed on the creation of an individual style.","cur":"dv:ART3","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"ceramics-1-2","title":"Ceramics 1-2","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course teaches hand building, glazing, and decorative techniques in clay.","cur":"dv:ART1","bank":"ceramics"},
{"id":"ceramics-3-4","title":"Ceramics 3-4","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Ceramics 1-2 or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course reinforces those skills and techniques used in Ceramics 1-2. Emphasis will be on both hand building and wheel techniques.","cur":"dv:ART2","bank":"ceramics"},
{"id":"ceramics-5-6","title":"Ceramics 5-6","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Ceramics 3-4 or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course reinforces skills and techniques used in Ceramics. Students will continue to explore hand building and wheel techniques.","cur":"dv:ART3","bank":"ceramics"},
{"id":"ceramics-portfolio-prep","title":"Ceramics Portfolio Prep","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Ceramics 5-6 or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course allows students to advance skills learned in prior classes and apply them in creating a coherent body of ceramics work. Students will prepare a portfolio for college and/or job interviews.","cur":"dv:ART3","bank":"ceramics"},
{"id":"3-dimensional-design","title":"3 Dimensional Design","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Art or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course will review the elements of design. The student will work with leather, jewelry, clay, weaving, and various mixed media projects.","cur":"dv:ART1","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"drawing-1-2","title":"Drawing 1-2","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Art or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course teaches the basic techniques of drawing through the use of several drawing media such as graphite, pen and ink, colored pencils, charcoal and pastels.","cur":"dv:ART1","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"drawing-3-4","title":"Drawing 3-4","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Drawing 1-2","desc":"This course offers exposure to drawing techniques. It is designed for students who wish to further their studies in drawing media such as pastels, pen and ink, pencil, mixed media, etc. This course is available for dual enrollment credit (ART 111) at some sites.","cur":"dv:ART2","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"painting-1-2","title":"Painting 1-2","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Art or Placement Criteria","desc":"Students will paint with a variety of painting media, such as acrylic, watercolor and oil, with an emphasis on art elements and principles. Color theory will be explored.","cur":"dv:ART1","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"painting-3-4","title":"Painting 3-4","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Painting 1-2","desc":"Students will be working in advanced painting techniques. An emphasis on individual style will be explored.","cur":"dv:ART2","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"studio-art-i-two-dimensional-art","title":"Studio Art I: Two Dimensional Art","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Art or Placement Criteria","desc":"Two Dimensional Art is a second year Fine Arts course that is a part of the IB MYP program. Students are required to take Intro to Art prior to taking this course. Students will be working primarily two-dimensionally. We practice developing skills that relate to drawing and painting, thinking creatively, knowing and understanding art terminology, and responding to works of art.","cur":"dv:ART2","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"studio-art-i-three-dimensional-art","title":"Studio Art I: Three Dimensional Art","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Art or Placement Criteria","desc":"Ceramics is a second year Fine Arts course that is a part of the IB MYP program. Students are required to take Intro to Art prior to taking this course. In this course, students will be working primarily three-dimensionally. We practice developing skills that relate to this medium, thinking creatively, knowing and understanding art terminology and vocabulary, and responding to works of art. Come join us in ceramics and get your creativity on!","cur":"dv:ART2","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"studio-art-ii","title":"Studio Art II","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Studio Art I","desc":"This course will emphasize broadening skills in both two-dimensional and three-dimensional art techniques, expanding student critical thinking and interpretation skills, and exploring art as a means of expression and cultural identity throughout the world.","cur":"dv:ART2","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"photography-and-design","title":"Photography and Design","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Art or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course will serve students who want to explore photography as an art form and for those interested in creative careers.","cur":"dv:ART1","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"2-d-portfolio-preparation","title":"2-D Portfolio Preparation","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Drawing 1-2 and Drawing 3-4 or Art 5-6","desc":"This is an advanced class designed for students who wish to prepare a portfolio for college or job interviews.","cur":"dv:ART3","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"3-d-portfolio-preparation","title":"3-D Portfolio Preparation","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"3-D Design or Instructor’s Signature","desc":"This advanced class is for students who wish to work in any three-dimensional area on a contracted, individualized basis. Students will prepare portfolios for college and/or job interviews.","cur":"dv:ART3","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"ap-studio-art-drawing","title":"AP Studio Art: Drawing","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Drawing 3-4 or Placement Criteria","desc":"The Drawing Portfolio is intended to address a very broad interpretation of drawing issues and media. Line quality, light and shade, rendering of form, composition, surface manipulation, the illusion of depth and mark-making are drawing issues that can be addressed through a variety of means, which could include painting, printmaking, mixed media, etc. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam.","cur":"dv:ART4","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"ap-studio-art-2d-design","title":"AP Studio Art: 2D Design","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"2-D Portfolio or Placement Criteria","desc":"The principles of design (unity/variety, balance, emphasis, contrast, rhythm, repetition, proportion/scale, figure/ground relationships) can be articulated through the visual elements (line, shape, color, value, texture, space). For this portfolio, students are asked to demonstrate mastery of 2-D design through any two-dimensional medium or process, including, but not limited to, graphic design, digital imaging, photography, collage, fabric design, weaving, fashion design, fashion illustration, painting and printmaking. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam.","cur":"dv:ART4","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"ap-studio-art-3d-design-ceramics","title":"AP Studio Art: 3D Design Ceramics","dept":"Visual Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"2-D Portfolio or Placement Criteria","desc":"In the 3-D Design Portfolio, students are asked to demonstrate their understanding of design principles as they relate to the integration of depth and space, volume and surface. The principles of design can be articulated through visual elements. For this portfolio, students are asked to demonstrate mastery of 3-D design through any three-dimensional approach, including, but not limited to, figurative or nonfigurative sculpture, architectural models, metal work, ceramics, glass work, installation, assemblage and 3-D fabric/fiber arts. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam.","cur":"dv:ART4","bank":"ceramics"},
{"id":"dance-fundamentals","title":"Dance Fundamentals","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This beginning level course develops techniques and routines in jazz, ballet, tap and modern dance. Basic anatomy, biology, kinesiology, dance history and terminology are studied. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:DANCE FUNDAMENTALS","bank":"dance"},
{"id":"intermediate-dance","title":"Intermediate Dance","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Dance Fundamentals or Audition","desc":"This intermediate level class is a continuation of Dance Fundamentals, using jazz, ballet, tap and modern dance forms. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:INTERMEDIATE DANCE","bank":"dance"},
{"id":"advanced-dance","title":"Advanced Dance","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Intermediate Dance or Audition","desc":"This course is designed to improve the techniques learned in previous classes and introduces higher level routines. Participation in nonschool hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:ADVANCED DANCE","bank":"dance"},
{"id":"dance-ensemble","title":"Dance Ensemble","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Dance or Audition","desc":"This advanced class is designed to improve techniques and performance abilities learned in earlier classes. All forms of dance will be taught. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:DANCE ENSEMBLE","bank":"dance"},
{"id":"performance-dance-ensemble","title":"Performance Dance Ensemble","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"This is a mastery level course where dancers are expected to have exemplary dance technique. Emphasis is placed on choreography and performance. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:PERFORMANCE DANCE ENSEMBLE","bank":"dance"},
{"id":"dance-for-musical-theater","title":"Dance for Musical Theater","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Dance for Musical Theater is a course designed to prepare students with the necessary dance training required for musical productions. This course focuses on building a strong foundation of traditional theater dance including tap, jazz and ballet basics as well as character and plot development of a story through dance. This class has no prerequisites and no experience necessary.","cur":"dv:DANCE FUNDAMENTALS","bank":"music"},
{"id":"dance-conditioning","title":"Dance Conditioning","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Using techniques inspired by yoga, Pilates, and ballet, this course is designed to improve muscular and postural strength, flexibility, alignment, balance, and body awareness in dancers. Students will also build their ability to focus, manage stress, achieve relaxation and enhance breath control. The course is taught and modified for each individual student’s needs and is beneficial to everyone from beginners to advanced practitioners. NOTE: This course does not count toward the Physical Education graduation requirement.","cur":"dv:DANCE FUNDAMENTALS","bank":"dance"},
{"id":"beginning-theatre","title":"Beginning Theatre","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is designed to acquaint students with acting, theatre production, theatre history, puppetry, pantomime, and dramatic activities.","cur":"dv:BEGINNING THEATRE","bank":"theatre"},
{"id":"intermediate-theatre","title":"Intermediate Theatre","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Theatre 1-2","desc":"This course helps students develop dramatic skills and acting techniques through participation in children’s theatre and one act plays.","cur":"dv:INTERMEDIATE THEATRE","bank":"theatre"},
{"id":"advanced-theatre","title":"Advanced Theatre","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Theatre 3-4","desc":"This advanced level course is an in depth study of the various aspects of the theatre and acting. Students will apply a variety of acting and directing techniques as they produce one act and full length plays. Outside class time may be required.","cur":"dv:ADVANCED THEATRE","bank":"theatre"},
{"id":"advanced-performance-theatre","title":"Advanced Performance Theatre","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Advanced Theatre or Audition","desc":"This in-depth study of acting, scene development and theatre production will enable students to select scripts for production and performance. The students will be responsible for all aspects of the production. Outside class time may be required.","cur":"dv:ADVANCED PERFORMANCE THEATRE","bank":"theatre"},
{"id":"stage-production-2","title":"Stage Production","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Theatre 1-2 or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course will introduce students to the technical and mechanical aspects of the theatre. Students will draft their own designs and construct stage scenery, as well as learn to use the lighting and sound equipment for the stage. Outside class time is required.","cur":"dv:STAGE PRODUCTION","bank":"theatre"},
{"id":"beginning-band","title":"Beginning Band","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This beginning band course teaches the fundamentals of playing a band instrument. No previous experience is necessary. This course addresses the music standards at the foundation level. Extra time outside of the regular school day is expected of all students for rehearsals and performances throughout the year.","cur":"dv:CONCERT BAND","bank":"music"},
{"id":"concert-band","title":"Concert Band","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is for the student who requires further training in music fundamentals in concert band literature. Participation in nonschool hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:CONCERT BAND","bank":"music"},
{"id":"band-color-guard","title":"Band – Color Guard","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is designed to provide students with instruction in the four elements of color guard including dance, flag, rifle, and saber. Students will also be expected to learn and use proper terminology and performance etiquette. Class activities will include performances, skill tests, and written assignments. All course objectives are aligned with Arizona College and Career Ready Standards and/or national standards and support school-wide efforts to increase student achievement.","cur":"dv:CONCERT BAND","bank":"music"},
{"id":"symphonic-band","title":"Symphonic Band","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"The course emphasizes concert performance in concert band literature. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:SYMPHONIC BAND","bank":"music"},
{"id":"wind-ensemble","title":"Wind Ensemble","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"This course emphasizes advanced concert band literature and performance. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances required.","cur":"dv:WIND ENSEMBLE"},
{"id":"jazz-ensemble","title":"Jazz Ensemble","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"This course emphasizes jazz performance in jazz band literature. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:SYMPHONIC BAND","bank":"music"},
{"id":"performance-jazz","title":"Performance Jazz","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"This course emphasizes advanced jazz literature and performance. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","cur":"dv:WIND ENSEMBLE","bank":"music"},
{"id":"percussion-ensemble","title":"Percussion Ensemble","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"This course emphasizes percussion performance. In addition to percussion ensemble literature, playing techniques for the primary instrument families will be dealt with including but not limited to snare drum, timpani, keyboard percussion, and auxiliary instruments. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances required.","cur":"dv:CONCERT BAND","bank":"music"},
{"id":"orchestra","title":"Orchestra","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is for the student who requires further training in music fundamentals in orchestral literature. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances is required.","bank":"music"},
{"id":"guitar-1-2","title":"Guitar 1-2","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This is a beginning course in basic guitar technique, chord and basic theory studies, note reading and chord studies. Students are requested to provide their own acoustic guitars.","bank":"music"},
{"id":"advanced-performance-guitar","title":"Advanced Performance Guitar","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Guitar 1-2/Audition or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course will continue to develop the techniques learned in Guitar 1-2. Students may perform at concerts throughout the year. Students are requested to provide their own acoustic guitars.","bank":"music"},
{"id":"music-theory","title":"Music Theory","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"1 Credit of a music performance class","desc":"This course introduces students to the basic elements of music theory. The course will stress writing skills, music analysis and ear training.","bank":"music"},
{"id":"ap-music-theory","title":"AP Music Theory","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"1 credit of a music performance class","desc":"This is a standard first-year college Music Theory and Composition course. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam.","bank":"music"},
{"id":"piano","title":"Piano","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course provides students with the opportunity to develop a working knowledge of the skills and techniques required to play the piano. Topics covered include music reading skills, correct keyboard fingering and technique, and general musicality. Students work individually at their own pace with teacher supervision and instruction. Keyboards with headphones are provided. Students will progress through instruction, practice, and performance.","bank":"music"},
{"id":"advanced-performance-piano","title":"Advanced Performance Piano","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course will continue to develop the techniques learned in the Piano class. Students may perform at concerts throughout the year. Students work individually at their own pace with teacher supervision and instruction. Keyboards with headphones are provided","bank":"music"},
{"id":"mariachi","title":"Mariachi","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course will focus on the fundamentals and skills of Mariachi music. No previous experience is necessary. Students will occasionally perform in public with the group and in small ensembles. Enrollment in the class obligates students to participate in all scheduled activities of the ensemble. Extra time outside of the regular school day is expected of all students for rehearsals and performances throughout the year. Students must complete semester one in order to participate in semester two, or with the approval of the instructor.","cur":"dv:CHORUS","bank":"music"},
{"id":"chorus","title":"Chorus","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course will provide students with an introduction to or continuing experience in choral singing and an opportunity to develop vocal technique, basic musicianship and performance skills. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances required.","cur":"dv:CHORUS","bank":"music"},
{"id":"concert-choir","title":"Concert Choir","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"This course will provide students with a challenging opportunity to continue their musical growth and development through choral singing. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances required.","cur":"dv:CONCERT CHOIR","bank":"music"},
{"id":"advanced-performance-choir","title":"Advanced Performance Choir","dept":"Performing Arts","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Audition","desc":"This course will provide students with a more advanced opportunity to continue their musical growth and development through choral singing. Participation in non-school hour rehearsals and performances required.","cur":"dv:ADVANCED PERFORMANCE CHOIR","bank":"music"},
{"id":"english-language-arts-1-2","title":"English Language Arts 1-2","dept":"English","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course introduces the student to the concepts and genres of literature. Students will develop skills in reading, writing, speaking, and listening aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts Standards. This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"ela9"},
{"id":"english-language-arts-1-2-h","title":"English Language Arts 1-2 H","dept":"English","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced-level course emphasizes the concepts and genres of literature and rhetoric and utilizes techniques from AP/IB curriculum. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts Standards as preparation for the AP or IB program in the 11th and 12th grades. Class requires summer reading and projects.","cur":"ela9"},
{"id":"english-language-arts-1-2-ib-myp-honors-gifted","title":"English Language Arts 1-2 IB MYP Honors/Gifted","dept":"English","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced course emphasizes the concepts and genres of literature and rhetoric, using a transdisciplinary and inquiry-driven approach. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities as preparation for the IB Diploma Programme in the 11th and 12th grades.","cur":"ela9"},
{"id":"english-language-arts-3-4","title":"English Language Arts 3-4","dept":"English","grade":"10","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"English Language Arts 1-2","desc":"This course introduces world literature and its geographic, ethnic, and historic influences. Students will develop skills in reading, writing, speaking and listening, and complete a research paper. . This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"ela10"},
{"id":"english-language-arts-3-4-ib-myp-honors-gifted","title":"English Language Arts 3-4 IB MYP Honors/Gifted","dept":"English","grade":"10","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"ELA 1-2 H and Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced course emphasizes the concepts and genres of literature and rhetoric, using a transdisciplinary and inquiry-driven approach. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities as preparation for the IB Diploma Programme in the 11th and 12th grades.","cur":"ela10"},
{"id":"ap-seminar-ela-h","title":"AP Seminar – ELA H","dept":"English","grade":"10","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"ELA 1-2 or 1-2 H","desc":"Students will develop and practice the skills in research, collaboration, and communication needed in any academic discipline. They will investigate topics in a variety of subject areas, write research-based essays, and design and give presentations both individually and as part of a team. Skills will focus on Reading and analyzing articles, studies, and other tests; Gathering and combining information from sources; Viewing an issue from multiple perspectives; Crafting arguments based on evidence. This course counts toward the 2 nd year ELA high school graduation credit."},
{"id":"english-language-arts-3-4-h","title":"English Language Arts 3-4 H","dept":"English","grade":"10","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"English Language Arts 1-2 H or Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced-level course introduces world literature and its geographic, ethnic, and historic influences. Students will use this literature to examine rhetorical strategies and practice advanced skills in communication. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts Standards as preparation for the AP or IB program in the 11th and 12th grades. Class requirements include summer reading and/or projects.","cur":"ela10"},
{"id":"english-language-arts-5-6","title":"English Language Arts 5-6","dept":"English","grade":"11","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"English Language Arts 3-4","desc":"This course introduces the literature and documents of America. Students will develop skills in reading, writing, speaking and listening aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts Standards. A research paper and a speech unit are included. This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"ela11"},
{"id":"ap-english-language-and-composition","title":"AP English Language and Composition","dept":"English","grade":"11","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"English Language Arts 3-4 H or Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced placement course includes a study of rhetoric and composition and follows the guidelines as set forth by the College Board. Students will develop skills in critical reading and analysis, writing, speaking and listening. Research activities and a speech unit are included. This course prepares students to take the Language and Composition AP exam. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. Class requirements include summer reading and/or projects. This course is aligned to ENG 101 / ENH 110 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","ap":"lang"},
{"id":"english-language-arts-7-8","title":"English Language Arts 7-8","dept":"English","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"English Language Arts 5-6","desc":"This course studies the comparative themes and literature of the world. Students will develop skills in reading, writing, speaking and listening aligned to the guidelines as set forth by the College Board. This course also will offer expanded opportunities to learn and practice essay strategies, including sentence/paragraph development. This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. . This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"ela12"},
{"id":"ap-english-literature-and-composition","title":"AP English Literature and Composition","dept":"English","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Jr. English Language Arts AP or Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced placement course includes a study of historic and current world literature and follows the guidelines as set forth by the College Board. Students will develop skills in critical reading and analysis, writing, speaking and listening. Research activities and a speech unit are included. This course prepares students to take the Literature and Composition AP exam. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. Class requirements include summer reading and/or projects.","ap":"lit"},
{"id":"ap-research-ela-7-8h","title":"AP Research ELA 7-8H","dept":"English","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AP Seminar","desc":"AP Research is an interdisciplinary course that encourages students to demonstrate critical thinking and academic research skills on a topic of the student’s choosing. Students build on what they learned in AP Seminar to deeply explore an academic topic, problem, or issue of individual interest. Through this exploration, they will design, plan and conduct a year-long research based investigation to address a research question. This course counts toward the 4 th year ELA graduation requirement."},
{"id":"english-language-development-eld","title":"English Language Development (ELD)","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"English Language Development is an intensive and prescriptive English program for students who are learning English as their second language. All students who qualify for the program will be placed in four courses of ELD based upon the proficiency level test results from the Arizona English Language Learner Assessment in oral, reading and writing components."},
{"id":"grammar-beginning","title":"Grammar-Beginning","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Grammar class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"grammar-basic","title":"Grammar-Basic","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Grammar-Beginning or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Grammar class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"grammar-intermediate","title":"Grammar-Intermediate","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Grammar-Basic or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Grammar class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"grammar-advanced","title":"Grammar-Advanced","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Grammar-Intermediate or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Grammar class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"oral-language-vocabulary-beginning","title":"Oral Language/Vocabulary-Beginning","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in an Oral Language/Vocabulary class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"oral-language-vocabulary-basic","title":"Oral Language/Vocabulary-Basic","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Oral Language/Vocabulary-Beginning or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in an Oral Language/Vocabulary class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"oral-language-vocabulary-intermediate","title":"Oral Language/Vocabulary-Intermediate","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Oral Language/Vocabulary-Basic or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in an Oral Language/Vocabulary class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"oral-language-vocabulary-advanced","title":"Oral Language/Vocabulary-Advanced","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Oral Language/Vocabulary-Intermediate or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in an Oral Language/Vocabulary class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"reading-beginning","title":"Reading-Beginning","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Reading class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"reading-basic","title":"Reading-Basic","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Reading-Beginning or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Reading class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"reading-intermediate","title":"Reading-Intermediate","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Reading-Basic or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Reading class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"reading-advanced","title":"Reading-Advanced","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Reading-Intermediate or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Reading class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"writing-beginning","title":"Writing-Beginning","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Writing class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"writing-basic","title":"Writing-Basic","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Writing-Beginning or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Writing class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"writing-intermediate","title":"Writing-Intermediate","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Writing-Basic or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Writing class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"writing-advanced","title":"Writing-Advanced","dept":"English Language Development","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Writing-Intermediate or AZELLA placement","desc":"Students will be placed initially in a Writing class based on AZELLA placement or annual spring reassessment. Students will progress in class level annually. Course work is based on the Arizona English Language Proficiency Standards, which are aligned to the Arizona English Language Arts standards."},
{"id":"english-language-arts-lab","title":"English Language Arts Lab","dept":"English","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This clinical course provides the skills to improve reading and/or writing proficiency. The emphasis may include word study, fluency, comprehension strategies, and composition strategies. Student AIMS results or student IEP are used to determine proficiency and may be used to determine course placement.","bank":"visual"},
{"id":"cinema-studies","title":"Cinema Studies","dept":"English","grade":"10-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course provides an introduction to the art, history, and theory of the cinema. Students will learn the grammar and structure of film while studying important genres and historic movements from the international cinema.","cur":"cinema"},
{"id":"communications-public-speaking","title":"Communications/Public Speaking","dept":"English","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course is for the student who wishes to explore 21st century communications, including multi-media presentations and speech delivery. Students gain confidence and skill through organizing, preparing and delivering various presentations for school and business world settings.","cur":"speech"},
{"id":"creative-writing","title":"Creative Writing","dept":"English","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is for the student who loves to write. Course emphasis will be on creative and original writings including short stories, poems, and drama. A major class project will be the production of a student literary magazine. This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","cur":"creative"},
{"id":"creative-writing-2","title":"Creative Writing 2","dept":"English","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Creative Writing 1","desc":"This semester-long course explores different genres of writing, through both reading and writing, as well as the other necessary elements needed to improve writing and composition skills. The course combines lecture, discussion, and workshop, leading to the production of marketable quality creative fiction and nonfiction. This course is designed with the goal of inspiring students to develop original pieces and ideas.","cur":"creative"},
{"id":"journalism","title":"Journalism","dept":"English","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course covers the fundamentals of journalistic writing and desktop publishing. Emphasis is placed on interviewing, various styles of writing, publication design, and press law. This course culminates in the publication of one issue of the school newspaper.","cur":"journalism"},
{"id":"newspaper","title":"Newspaper","dept":"English","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Journalism or Placement Criteria","desc":"This is a publication class for students working on the newspaper staff as editors, reporters, and photographers. Training on computers will acquaint students with desktop publishing technology.","cur":"journalism"},
{"id":"yearbook","title":"Yearbook","dept":"English","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This is a publication class for students on the yearbook staff. The staff reports, writes, and edits written material, coordinates all photos, designs and executes all page layouts. The staff is also responsible for the annual publication of the school yearbook.","cur":"journalism"},
{"id":"algebra-1-2","title":"Algebra 1-2","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9-10","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course includes formal mathematical concepts such as polynomial operations, linear equations, and the quadratic equation, as well as the study of statistics and geometry from an algebraic perspective. Emphasis is placed on studying algebra in context through problem solving and real-life applications. This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"alg1"},
{"id":"algebra-1-2-h","title":"Algebra 1-2 H","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement test or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course provides a more rigorous approach to the topics covered in Algebra 1-2. Students should be proficient in operations with rational numbers, properties of equality, and single transformation equations. Students who have received credit in Algebra 1-2 in 7th or 8th grade do not qualify for this course.","cur":"alg1"},
{"id":"algebra-1-2-ib-myp-honors-gifted","title":"Algebra 1-2 IB MYP Honors/Gifted","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course provides a more rigorous approach to the algebra topics. Students should be proficient in operations with rational numbers, properties of equality, and single transformation equations. Students who have received credit in Algebra 1-2 in 7th or 8th grade do not qualify for this course. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities as preparation for the IB Diploma Programme in the 11th and 12th grades.","cur":"alg1"},
{"id":"geometry-1-2","title":"Geometry 1-2","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 1-2","desc":"This course deals with plane and solid geometry as well as transformational geometry and their applications. Geometry will be studied from an algebraic and non-algebraic perspective, including strategies on geometric proofs. . This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"geo"},
{"id":"geometry-1-2-h","title":"Geometry 1-2 H","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 1-2 H or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course involves a more rigorous analysis of the topics covered in geometry and includes the study of non-Euclidean geometries. Emphasis will be placed on developing deductive clarity and innovation.","cur":"geo"},
{"id":"geometry-1-2-ib-myp-honors-gifted","title":"Geometry 1-2 IB MYP Honors/Gifted","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9-10","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 1-2 H and Placement Criteria","desc":"This course involves a more rigorous analysis of the topics covered in geometry and includes the study of non-Euclidean geometries. Emphasis will be placed on developing deductive clarity and innovation.. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities as preparation for the IB Diploma Programme in the 11th and 12th grades.","cur":"geo"},
{"id":"algebra-applications","title":"Algebra Applications","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 1-2 and Geometry","desc":"This course includes the study of sequences and series, functions and their inverses, complex numbers and radical, quadratic, rational and exponential functions. Students should be proficient with linear equations and prepared to study math in context. NOTE: Algebra Applications do not fulfill university admission requirements. . This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"collegealg"},
{"id":"algebra-3-4","title":"Algebra 3-4","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 1-2 and Geometry","desc":"This course includes the study of algebraic expressions, functions and relations, systems of equations and inequalities including compound inequalities, exponential and logarithmic equations, polynomial, rational, radical and exponential functions as well as the graphs of absolute value and piece-wise functions. Success in this course will prepare students for entry into Precalculus, college algebra, college math, trigonometry, or probability and statistics.","cur":"alg2"},
{"id":"algebra-3-4-h","title":"Algebra 3-4 H","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 1-2 H and Geometry H or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course involves a more rigorous analysis of the topics covered in Algebra 3-4 and includes a study of trigonometry, matrices, and solutions to higher-order relations.","cur":"alg2"},
{"id":"financial-math","title":"Financial Math","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Algebra Applications or Algebra 3-4","desc":"This course includes the study of the complex financial world students will encounter in their lives including taxes, bankruptcy, investing, hedge funds as well as personal financial topics like loans, (car, college, home and pay day loans), insurance, credit card purchases, loan refinancing, financial software, budgeting, and retirement planning. This class is a project based course. . This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"finmath"},
{"id":"financial-math-applications","title":"Financial Math Applications","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"12","length":"1 Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra Applications or Algebra 3-4 AND Active IEP","desc":"This course includes the study of the financial world students will encounter in their lives including: the difference between saving and checking accounts, all aspects of taxes, bankruptcy, investing hedge funds as well as personal financial topics like loans (car, college, home and payday loans), all types of insurance, credit card purchases, loan refinancing, financial software, budgeting, retirement planning, entrepreneurship, consumer skills, and finding the right job from resume to being hired. This class is a project-based course. NOTE: Financial Math Applications does not fulfill university admission requirements. This course is a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services, as designed on the student’s IEP.","cur":"finmath"},
{"id":"probability-and-statistics","title":"Probability and Statistics","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Algebra 3-4 or Algebra Applications","desc":"This course is designed to develop a greater understanding and appreciation for and skill in applying statistical techniques in the decision-making process. Topics include: descriptive statistics, probability, and statistical inference. Practical examples based on real data are used throughout the course. Students will plan and conduct experiments or surveys and analyze the resulting data. . This course can be offered as a Learning Center Content (LCC) course for students with disabilities who are eligible for special education services as designated on the student’s IEP.","cur":"stats"},
{"id":"college-math","title":"College Math","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Algebra 3-4 or Algebra Applications","desc":"This course includes the study of topics traditionally not included in algebra based courses. Topics include set theory, logic, counting methods, probability, statistics, and personal financial management. This course is aligned to MAT 142 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","cur":"collegealg"},
{"id":"college-algebra","title":"College Algebra","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Algebra 3-4","desc":"This course includes the study of direct, inverse, joint, and indirect variation, linear regression, functions and relations, systems of both linear and non-linear equations, conics, matrices, logarithmic functions, exponential growth and decay, radical and rational expressions and equations, domain, range, and polynomial expressions, equations and graphs. This course is aligned to MAT 151 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","cur":"collegealg"},
{"id":"trigonometry","title":"Trigonometry","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Algebra 3-4","desc":"This course includes the study of right triangle trigonometry, graphs, the law of sines and cosines, unit circle, trigonometric functions and their graphs, inverse trigonometric functions and their graphs, and trigonometric forms of complex numbers. This course is aligned to MAT 182 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","cur":"trig"},
{"id":"precalculus-h","title":"Precalculus H","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is designed to develop an abstract understanding of a variety of mathematical topics, including trigonometry, limiting processes, logarithms, vectors, and matrices. This course is aligned to MAT 152/182 at the community college and may be offered for dual enrollment credit as MAT 187 or MAT 152/182. Community college tuition applies.","cur":"precalc"},
{"id":"statistics","title":"Statistics","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course includes basic concepts and applications of statistics, including data description, estimation and hypothesis tests.","cur":"stats"},
{"id":"statistical-reasoning-in-sports","title":"Statistical Reasoning in Sports","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":"1","desc":"This course will introduce students to statistical reasoning in the context of sports. Statistical concepts such as exploratory data analysis, hypothesis testing, experimental design and probability will be developed to answer interesting sports related questions. Students analyze data, perform simulations, and draw conclusions to develop an understanding of statistics.","cur":"stats"},
{"id":"ap-statistics","title":"AP Statistics","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This is a standard college Probability and Statistics course. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam.","ap":"stats"},
{"id":"ap-precalculus","title":"AP Precalculus","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 3-4 H or Placement Criteria","desc":"Through the course, students strengthen their procedural and symbolic fluency skills needed for higher level mathematics. While studying each function type, students solve equations and construct equivalent analytic representations in both contextual and purely mathematical settings.","ap":"precalc"},
{"id":"ap-calculus-ab","title":"AP Calculus Ab","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This is a standard college Calculus course. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. Differentiation and integration involving polynomial, exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric functions with practical applications will be a major part of the course content. This course is aligned to MAT 221 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","ap":"calcab"},
{"id":"ap-calculus-bc","title":"AP Calculus Bc","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria ELECTIVES (Not for Mathematics Credit)","desc":"This is the second year of the standard college Calculus course. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam and may receive both AB and BC credit. This course is aligned to MAT231 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","ap":"calcbc"},
{"id":"algebra-foundations","title":"Algebra Foundations","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is reserved for students requiring additional intervention in math. The focus will be to raise the competence and confidence of first-year algebra students by equipping them with a set of broadly applicable tools and strategies to help them make sense of what they are learning in algebra. This course provides support for students in meeting proficiency levels in the algebra state standards.","cur":"alg1"},
{"id":"math-lab","title":"Math Lab","dept":"Mathematics","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This math lab is reserved for students requiring additional intervention in math. The focus will be to reinforce and practice skills. This course provides support for students in meeting proficiency levels in state math standards.","cur":"alg1"},
{"id":"racquet-individual-sports","title":"Racquet/ Individual Sports","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This is a fitness concept based class that includes activities such as badminton, racquetball, tennis and individual sports.","cur":"dv:RACQUET/ INDIVIDUAL SPORTS","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"fitness-center","title":"Fitness Center","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This is a fitness concept based class that focuses on cardiovascular exercises such as aerobic dance, treadmills, stationary bikes, Stairmaster, line dancing, plyometric conditioning, running, and speed walking.","cur":"dv:FITNESS CENTER","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"team-sports","title":"Team Sports","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This is a fitness concept based class that includes activities such as basketball, volleyball, soccer, field hockey, ultimate Frisbee, and Speedaway.","cur":"dv:TEAM SPORTS","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"general-physical-education","title":"General Physical Education","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-10","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course develops the whole individual; physically, intellectually, emotionally and socially. As students progress, they encounter concepts and information across a spectrum of sports and activities. They are challenged to not only internalize this knowledge, but to competently apply it, using effective communication modes and through performance of various strategies, tactics, skills and techniques. Students will have the opportunity to participate in various activities, such as Basketball, Tennis, Volleyball, Racquetball, Flag Football, Badminton, Soccer, Softball, Lacrosse, etc.","cur":"dv:GENERAL PHYSICAL EDUCATION","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"limited-physical-education","title":"Limited Physical Education","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Physician or School Nurse Approval","desc":"This class is for those students unable to take Physical Education due to health problems. This course meets the physical education portion of the graduation requirement. Core class.","cur":"dv:LIMITED PHYSICAL EDUCATION","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"health","title":"Health","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"None ELECTIVES (Not for Physical Education Credit) The three courses are non-elective courses.","desc":"Health will provide students with the skills and knowledge to make personal health decisions and maintain a healthy environment. Meets the health portion of the graduation requirement. Required with a physical education core class. CPR instruction provided in this course.","cur":"dv:HEALTH","bank":"health"},
{"id":"advanced-physical-education","title":"Advanced Physical Education","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"PE Core class","desc":"Students have the opportunity to expand on concepts of core classes while participating in individual and team sports such as racquetball, basketball, volleyball, tennis and softball.","cur":"dv:ADVANCED PHYSICAL EDUCATION","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"introduction-to-weight-training","title":"Introduction to Weight Training","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course emphasizes weight training using fixed and free weights. Students will study the muscular system and participate in aerobic activities for endurance.","cur":"dv:INTRODUCTION TO WEIGHT TRAINING","bank":"strength"},
{"id":"advanced-weight-training","title":"Advanced Weight Training","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Introduction to Weight Training or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is for those students interested in serious weight training for bodybuilding, athletics, or competitive lifting. Each student will develop and carry out an individualized conditioning program.","cur":"dv:ADVANCED WEIGHT TRAINING","bank":"strength"},
{"id":"power-training","title":"Power Training","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Weight Training 1-2 or Instructor Approval","desc":"This is a strength and conditioning class that will focus on training principles and nutritional strategies designed to enhance student performance and health. The course involves cardiovascular training, agility training, plyometric conditioning, speed development, hand eye coordination, flexibility and resistance training.","cur":"dv:POWER TRAINING","bank":"strength"},
{"id":"advanced-fitness-center","title":"Advanced Fitness Center","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"10-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"PE Core class","desc":"This course will include an intense program in physical fitness through warm-up, cardiovascular, and cool-down activities. This class will also include strengthening and toning activities. Students will also learn about muscles, nutrition, and weight control.","cur":"dv:ADVANCED FITNESS CENTER","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"lifetime-sports","title":"Lifetime Sports","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"10-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"PE Core class","desc":"This course builds on fitness concepts in activities that will include croquet, speed walking, golf, Frisbee, and table tennis.","cur":"dv:LIFETIME SPORTS","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"unified-sports","title":"Unified Sports","dept":"Physical Education","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course is based on the Special Olympics Sports Program. It combines students in a specialized program with students in general education for sports training and competition. Sports include basketball, flag football, floor hockey, soccer, volleyball, and others. The course may be repeated for credit.","cur":"dv:TEAM SPORTS","bank":"pe"},
{"id":"biology","title":"Biology","dept":"Science","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This lab course concentrates on the study of living organisms. Labs are designed to investigate objectives presented in class. This course meets the requirement for graduation of one Lab Science being Biology. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.E1U3.14, HS.L2U3.18, HS.L2U1.19, HS.L1U1.20, HS.L2U1.21, HS.L1U1.22, HS.L1U3.23, HS.L3U1.24, HS.L3U1.25, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.27, HS.L4U1.28","cur":"biology"},
{"id":"biology-h","title":"Biology H","dept":"Science","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This lab course will cover in-depth topics such as cell structure, genetics and ecology. Labs are designed to improve student skills and research projects are required. This course meets the requirement for graduation of one Lab Science being Biology. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.E1U3.14, HS.E2U1.17 , HS.L2U3.18, HS.L2U1.19, HS.L1U1.20, HS.L2U1.21, HS.L1U1.22, HS.L1U3.23, HS.L3U1.24, HS.L3U1.25, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.27, HS.L4U1.28","cur":"biology"},
{"id":"biology-1-2-ib-myp-honors-gifted","title":"Biology 1-2 IB MYP Honors/Gifted","dept":"Science","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This lab course will cover in-depth topics such as cell structure, genetics and ecology. Labs are designed to improve student skills and research projects are required. This course meets the requirement for graduation of one Lab Science being Biology. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities as preparation for the IB Diploma Programme in the 11th and 12th grades. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.E1U3.14, HS.E2U1.17 , HS.L2U3.18, HS.L2U1.19, HS.L1U1.20, HS.L2U1.21, HS.L1U1.22, HS.L1U3.23, HS.L3U1.24, HS.L3U1.25, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.27, HS.L4U1.28","cur":"biology"},
{"id":"chemistry","title":"Chemistry","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Biology and Algebra 1-2","desc":"This lab class will study the composition of materials. Areas covered include the basic structure of atoms, how atoms combine to produce molecules, and how symbols and formulas are used to write chemical equations. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.1, HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E2U1.15, HS.E2U1.17","cur":"chemistry"},
{"id":"chemistry-h","title":"Chemistry H","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Passed Biology or Biology H & Algebra 1-2 or Algebra 1-2 H","desc":"This advanced level lab course includes more detailed study of concepts and a heavier emphasis on mathematics. It concentrates on the theoretical explanation of materials. This course is aligned to CHM 130 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.1, HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E2U1.15, HS.E2U1.17","cur":"chemistry"},
{"id":"earth-science","title":"Earth Science","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This laboratory class is designed to introduce students to the foundations of Earth Sciences. Students will study erosion, plate tectonics, meteorology, astronomy, and the geologic time scale. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.1, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U3.9, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E1U1.11, HS.E1U1.12, HS.E1U1.13, HS.E2U1.15, HS.E2U1.16, HS.E2U1.17","cur":"earth"},
{"id":"earth-science-h","title":"Earth Science H","dept":"Science","grade":"9-11","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Passed Biology or Biology H HONORS EARTH SCIENCE (accelerated course) the essential standards for Earth and Space Science.","desc":"This laboratory class analyzes and describes Earth’s interconnected systems and how they are changing due to natural processes and human influence. Topics covered include rocks, minerals, sculpting of Earth’s surface, plate tectonics, earthquakes, volcanoes, geologic history, the atmosphere, weather, climate, the solar system, stars, and galaxies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U3.9, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E1U1.11, HS.E1U1.12, HS.E1U1.13, HS.E2U1.15, HS.E2U1.16, HS.E2U1.17","cur":"earth"},
{"id":"environmental-science","title":"Environmental Science","dept":"Science","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Biology or Biology H","desc":"This is an applied lab science based on experimentation and observation. Students are expected to solve environmental problems that relate to resource depletion, pollution and extinction. This science takes a global perspective focusing on both developed and developing countries. This course is aligned to BIO 105 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.1, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U3.9, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E1U1.11, HS.E1U1.12, HS.E1U1.13, HS.E1U3.14, HS.E2U1.15, HS.E2U1.16, HS.E2U1.17","cur":"envsci"},
{"id":"environmental-science-h","title":"Environmental Science H","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Biology 1-2 or Biology 1-2H","desc":"This lab class will examine the mutual relationships between organisms and their environment. In studying the interrelationships among plants, animals, and humans, this course will cover the following subjects: photosynthesis, recycling and regeneration, ecosystems, population and growth studies, pollution, and conservation of natural resources. This honors level course will allow students to engage with the principles, concepts, and methodologies at a more in-depth and rigorous level. This course is aligned to BIO 105 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.1, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E1U1.11, HS.E1U1.12, HS.E1U1.13, HS.E1U3.14, HS.E2U1.15, HS.E2U1.16, HS.E2U1.17, HS.L2U1.19, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.27.","cur":"envsci"},
{"id":"anatomy-physiology","title":"Anatomy/Physiology","dept":"Science","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Biology plus one other high school lab science","desc":"This is a lab-oriented course that deals with the structure and function of the major human body systems. This course is aligned to BIO 160 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.3, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P4U1.8, HS.L1U1.20, HS.L2U1.21, HS.L1U1.22, HS.L1U3.23, HS.L3U1.24, HS.L3U1.25, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.28","cur":"anatomy"},
{"id":"anatomy-physiology-h","title":"Anatomy/Physiology H","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Bio/Bio H, Earth Sci/Earth Sci H/H Earth Sci or Env Sci/ Env Sci.H or 1 Sem Earth H + Chem/Chem H High School Science Course Sequence For Class Year 2023 and later th th th Biology or Honors Biology or BIO 100 Environmental Science Chemistry, Honors Chemistry, CHEM 130 Physics/Honors Physics AP Physics/PHY 101 Anatomy/BIO 160 Forensics Marine Biology Physics/Honors Physics AP Physics/PHY 101 AP Environmental Science/BIO 105 Forensics Marine Biology Anatomy/BIO 160 Earth Science or Honors Earth Science th th 1 Semester Module 11 th Honors Biology or BIO 100 Honors Chemistry H Earth Science AP Chemistry AP Biology AP Environmental Science/BIO 15","desc":"This lab-oriented course deals with the structure and function of the major human body systems, including histology, cells, tissues, organ systems, respiration circulation, digestion, endocrine function, and other key body functions. This course requires students to apply their knowledge of anatomy and physiology to real-world situations and use critical thinking skills. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.3,HS.P1U3.4,HS.P4U1.8, HS.L1U1.20, HS.L2U1.21, HS.L1U1.22, HS.L1U3.23, HS.L3U1.24, HS.L3U1.25, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.28.","cur":"anatomy"},
{"id":"chem-151-152","title":"Chem 151/152","dept":"Science","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Algebra 3-4 and two years high school lab science","desc":"Honors Physics/AP Physics/PHY101 BIO 145/Marine Biology BIO 160 IB Biology IB Chemistry Forensics Anatomy Environmental Science ASTRONOMY 1-2 This upper level lab course introduces students to Earth-Moon-sun dynamics, origin and structure of the Solar System focusing on planets, dwarf planets, and lesser celestial bodies. In addition, students will be introduced to topics in stellar evolution, galaxies, the Big Bang theory, and the history of human space exploration.","cur":"chemistry"},
{"id":"forensic-science","title":"Forensic Science","dept":"Science","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Biology plus one other high school lab science","desc":"This lab course concentrates on the application of science to civil and criminal laws that are enforced by law enforcement agencies in a criminal justice system. Labs are designed to collect and analyze evidence. Some material presented in this class is of a sensitive nature and may be difficult for some individuals. Student discretion is advised. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.3, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U1.10, HS.L3U1.24","cur":"forensic"},
{"id":"marine-biology","title":"Marine Biology","dept":"Science","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Biology plus one other high school lab science","desc":"Marine Biology is a survey of marine environments and their biotic communities with an emphasis on the natural history of marine organisms. This is a lab science that will focus on inquiry and problem-solving through the lens of marine interactions. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E1U3.14, HS.L2U3.18, HS.L2U1.19, HS.L1U1.20, HS.L1U1.22, HS.L3U1.24, HS.L3U1.25, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.27, HS.L4U1.28","cur":"marine"},
{"id":"physics","title":"Physics","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"2 years of high school science and have taken or are currently enrolled in Algebra 3-4","desc":"This course is a laboratory oriented scientific investigation of mechanics, heat, optics, sound, light, electromagnetism, nuclear energy, and atomic structure. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.E2U1.16","cur":"physics"},
{"id":"physics-h","title":"Physics H","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Passed 2 years of high school science and have taken or are currently enrolled in Algebra 3-4","desc":"This course is a lab science that is an integration of the conceptual approach and mathematical bases presentation of Newtonian Mechanics, Fluid Mechanics, Thermodynamics, Oscillatory Motion, Sound, Electricity and Light Energy. The emphasis is on implementation of problem solving skills, lab skills and understanding. This course is aligned to PHY 101 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.E2U1.16","cur":"physics"},
{"id":"ap-biology","title":"AP Biology","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Passed Biology or Biology H RECOMMENDED: Chemistry or Chemistry H","desc":"This is a lab oriented course for students who have demonstrated achievement in biology and chemistry and desire to do college-level work. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. This course is aligned to BIO 101 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.1, HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U1.10, HS.E1U1.13, HS.E1U3.14, HS.L2U1.19, HS.L1U1.20, HS.L2U1.21, HS.L1U1.22, HS.L1U3.23, HS.L3U1.24, HS.L3U1.25, HS.L3U3.26, HS.L4U1.27, HS.L4U1.28","ap":"bio"},
{"id":"ap-chemistry","title":"AP Chemistry","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Passed Chemistry, Chemistry H and have taken","desc":"This lab course is for students who wish to pursue a college level chemistry course. This course follows the Advanced Placement Chemistry Program. The lab work includes exercises in both qualitative and quantitative analysis. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. This course is aligned to CHM 151/152 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.1, HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U1.10","ap":"chem"},
{"id":"ap-environmental-science","title":"AP Environmental Science","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Passed Biology or Biology H plus one other high school lab science","desc":"This is a lab-oriented course for students who have demonstrated achievement in their two previous years of science and desire to do college level work. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. This course is aligned to BIO 105 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P1U1.2, HS.P1U1.3, HS.P1U3.4, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.E1U1.11, HS.E1U3.14, HS.L2U3.18, HS.L2U1.19, HS.L3U1.25","ap":"apes"},
{"id":"ap-physics-i","title":"AP Physics I","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Enrollment in Algebra 3-4","desc":"This course is a laboratory oriented scientific investigations course that focuses on inquiry-based instruction and in-depth exploration of the following topics: Newtonian mechanics (including rotational dynamics and angular momentum); work, energy, and power; mechanical waves and sound. It will also introduce electric circuits. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. This course is aligned to PHY 101 at the community college and is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies. This course covers the following Arizona essential standards: HS.P2U1.5, HS.P3U1.6, HS.P3U2.7, HS.P4U1.8, HS.P4U3.9, HS.P4U1.10","ap":"phys1"},
{"id":"ap-physics-ii","title":"AP Physics II","dept":"Science","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Passed Algebra 3-4 or Algebra 3-4 H and AP Physics I","desc":"This course is a laboratory oriented scientific investigations course that focuses on inquiry-based instruction and in-depth exploration of the following topics: fluid mechanics; thermodynamics; electricity and magnetism; optics; atomic and nuclear physics. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. This course is aligned to PHY 111/112.","ap":"phys2"},
{"id":"ap-physics-c-mechanics","title":"AP Physics C: Mechanics","dept":"Science","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Calculus AB","desc":"The AP Physics C: Mechanics course explores concepts such as kinematics; Newton's laws of motion, work, energy, and power; systems of particles and linear momentum; rotation; oscillations; and gravitation. The class includes hands-on laboratory work and inclass activities to investigate phenomena and use calculus to solve problems.","ap":"physcm"},
{"id":"world-history","title":"World History","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"10","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"Through the process of inquiry students will explore people, events and movements in World History with a comprehensive study of the impact of social, geographic, political, and economic influences on historical events and geography. This course includes the study of the people of Africa, the Americas, Asia, and Europe with a balanced approach to the Eastern and Western Hemispheres. The course will begin with the 15th century to allow for depth of content and connection to current issues and events. The course will include the following topics of study: world belief systems, interregional interactions, revolutions of thought, industrial revolution, political revolutions, global rise of nation-states, World War I through World War II, Holocaust and genocides, Cold War, global imperialism, and contemporary global issues. Students will be able to apply the lessons of world history to their lives as citizens of the United States and members of the world community. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for World History/Geography. NOTE: This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites.","cur":"world"},
{"id":"world-history-h","title":"World History H","dept":"Social Studies","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"IB Middle Years Programme Student (Year 4","desc":"This rigorous course covers the same concepts as World History but is designed for advanced students looking for more rigor and complexity of content knowledge and disciplinary skills. Students will trace the development of civilizations from early prehistoric people through the present. Emphasis will be placed on students doing the work of historians, geographers, political scientists, and economists to develop a deeper understanding of content and a strong connection to the material Students may be required to complete an outside research project during the course of the year. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for World History/Geography NOTE: This is a weighted course.","cur":"world"},
{"id":"ap-world-history-modern","title":"AP World History: Modern","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is designed for motivated students and equivalent to a two-semester introductory college or university world history course. Study the cultural, economic, political, and social developments that have shaped the world from c. 1200 CE to the present through analyzing historical sources, learning to make connections, and crafting historical arguments as students explore concepts like humans and the environment, cultural developments and interactions, governance, economic systems, social interactions and organization, and technology and innovation. College Board has outlined the curricular requirements for this class NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for World History/Geography or may be taken for elective credit. NOTE: This is a weighted course NOTE: Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam in May","ap":"world"},
{"id":"united-states-history","title":"United States History","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"11","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"Using inquiry in history, high school students will explore a variety of peoples, events, and movements in United States history with a focus on inquiry into the evolution of American democratic principles, changes in society, economic and geographical development, and the emergence of the United States as a global power. The course will include content from the following historical eras: Revolution and a New Nation, nation building and sectionalism, Civil War and Reconstruction, emergence of modern America, Great Depression and World War II, postwar United States, and contemporary United States. Special attention will be paid to how Arizona and its diverse cultures and individuals contribute to United States history from the colonial period through the Cold War. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for American History, including Arizona history. NOTE: Optional college credit may be available; dual enrollment fee required.","cur":"us"},
{"id":"ap-united-states-history","title":"AP United States History","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"11","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is designed for motivated students and equivalent to a two-semester introductory college or university U.S. history course. Students cultivate their understanding of U.S. history from c. 1491 CE to the present through analyzing historical sources and learning to make connections and craft historical arguments as they explore concepts like American and national identity; work, exchange, and technology; geography and the environment; migration and settlement; politics and power; America in the world; American and regional culture; and social structures. Class requirements may include summer readings and projects. This course is designed to prepare enrolled students for the AP exam that they are expected to take in May. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for American History, including Arizona history. NOTE: This is a weighted course NOTE: Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam in May","ap":"apush"},
{"id":"government","title":"Government","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Using inquiry in civics, high school students explore how to become active citizens. To become engaged citizens requires a knowledge of the history, principles, and foundations of our republic. A comprehensive study of civics can be approached from many angles and perspectives with a focus on inquiry. Eight to ten hours of community service will be required. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for American Government, including Arizona government. NOTE: This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites. Community college tuition applies.","cur":"gov"},
{"id":"economics","title":"Economics","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Using inquiry in economics, high school students explore the economic reasoning process to make informed decisions in a wide variety of contexts. Economics is grounded in knowledge about how people, institutions, and societies choose to use resources to meet their wants and needs. The study of economics can be approached from many angles and perspectives with a focus on inquiry. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for Economic/Free Enterprise.","cur":"econ"},
{"id":"ap-united-states-government-and-politics","title":"AP United States Government and Politics","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course meets the graduation requirement. It will prepare students to take the Advanced Placement Exams. The course provides the students with an overview and analysis of the principles and practices of the American political and governmental systems. Class requirements may include summer reading, projects and 8 hours of community service. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for American Government, including Arizona government. NOTE: Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam","ap":"gov"},
{"id":"ap-micro-economics","title":"AP Micro-Economics","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"The course is designed to help students develop critical thinking through the understanding, application, and analysis of fundamental economic concepts. Students will be expected to apply quantitative and mathematical skills to economics as well as apply economic logic to a wide variety of real-world and hypothetical situations. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for Economics/Free Enterprise","ap":"micro"},
{"id":"ap-macro-economics","title":"AP Macro-Economics","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"Placement Criteria ELECTIVES (Not for Social Studies Credit)","desc":"The purpose of an AP course in macroeconomics is to give students a thorough understanding of the principles of economics that apply to an economic system as a whole. The course places particular emphasis on the study of national income and price-level determination and also develops students’ familiarity with economic performance measures, the financial sector, stabilization policies, economic growth and international economics. NOTE: This course meets the high school graduation requirements for Economics/Free Enterprise NOTE: This course may also be taken for elective credit.","ap":"macro"},
{"id":"us-1-h-academy","title":"US 1 H (Academy)","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course focuses on the native civilizations, colonization, and the founding documents, words, and people of the American Revolution ending with the Early Nation period.","cur":"us"},
{"id":"us-2-h-academy","title":"US 2 H (Academy)","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"10","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"US1 H","desc":"This course focuses on the early American republic, the antebellum period, territorial expansion, and the development of the American West.","cur":"us"},
{"id":"us-3-h-academy","title":"US 3 H (Academy)","dept":"Social Studies","see":"AP United States History","desc":"","cur":"us"},
{"id":"us-4-h-academy","title":"US 4 H (Academy)","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"US 2 H/AP US History","desc":"In this course students will match their personal interests to a community sponsor to complete community service and research.","cur":"us"},
{"id":"introduction-to-criminal-justice-and-constitutional-law","title":"Introduction to Criminal Justice and Constitutional Law","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course is an introduction to crime and society's responses to it. Students will examine the nature and causes of crime, the criminal law, constitutional safeguards, and the organization and operation of the criminal justice system including the police, courts, jails, prisons. Topics include statutory law and judicial decisions governing the areas of arrest, search and seizure, interrogations and confessions, self-incrimination and other constitutional guarantees.","cur":"law"},
{"id":"law-in-society","title":"Law in Society","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course studies the American legal system and the problems and issues it encounters. Topics include criminal law, civil law, juvenile law, family law, consumer law, and environmental law and mock trials.","cur":"law"},
{"id":"sociology","title":"Sociology","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course is for students who are interested in studying problems and issues in today’s society. Subject matter will explore family, race relations, crime, religion, sex roles, dating, law, death, and other human interactions. The course requires reading and special projects. This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites.","cur":"sociology"},
{"id":"psychology","title":"Psychology","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Students will explore such topics as human growth and development, the development of personality and intelligence, principles of learning, frustration and conflict, and personality disturbances and their treatment. This course is available for dual enrollment credit at some sites.","cur":"psych"},
{"id":"modern-american-history","title":"Modern American History","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"The course will examine the major social, political and economic events of the 1960’s–today. Emphasis will be placed on the Civil Rights Movement, the J.F.K. Assassination, the Vietnam War, the Watergate Scandals and the Counter Culture Movement, and Terrorism.","cur":"us"},
{"id":"ap-european-history","title":"AP European History","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AP or Honors World","desc":"Students in this course are expected to demonstrate knowledge of basic chronology and major events and trends from the High Renaissance of approximately 1450 to the present. The broad themes of intellectual-cultural, political-diplomatic, and social economic history form the basis of the course within that chronology.","ap":"euro"},
{"id":"ap-comparative-government-and-politics","title":"AP Comparative Government and Politics","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AP Government","desc":"The AP course in Comparative Government and Politics introduces students to fundamental concepts used by political scientists to study the processes and outcomes of politics in a variety of country settings. The course should cover specific countries and their governments.","ap":"compgov"},
{"id":"ap-psychology","title":"AP Psychology","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"The AP Psychology course is designed to introduce students to the systematic and scientific study of the behavior and mental processes of human beings and other animals. Students are exposed to the psychological facts, principles, and phenomena associated with each of the major subfields within psychology. They also learn about the ethics and methods psychologists use in their science and practice. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam.","ap":"psych"},
{"id":"anthropology","title":"Anthropology","dept":"Social Studies","credit":".5","desc":"This course surveys and explains the cultural, linguistic and biological legacy of humankind, from antiquity to the present, using the research tools of anthropology. It presents students with a grounding in the four major subfields: archaeology, biological anthropology, cultural anthropology, and linguistics. Special topics include ancient civilizations, forensics, osteology, primatology, art history, and magic.","cur":"anthropology"},
{"id":"ap-human-geography","title":"AP Human Geography","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course introduces students to the systematic study of patterns and processes that have shaped human understanding, use, and alteration of the Earth’s surface. Students employ spatial concepts and landscape analysis to examine socioeconomic organization and its environmental consequences. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam.","ap":"hug"},
{"id":"world-war-ii-history","title":"World War II History","dept":"Social Studies","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course examines the complex causes, pivotal events, and enduring legacy of World War II. Students will explore topics such as the Treaty of Versailles, totalitarianism, major battles, the Holocaust, and atomic warfare. Students will gain insight into how World War II continues to shape the modern world. The course emphasizes historical thinking, critical analysis, and empathy.","cur":"ww2"},
{"id":"american-sign-language-1-2","title":"American Sign Language 1-2","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is an introduction to American Sign Language (ASL) and deaf culture. Students learn basic comprehension and expression through conversation of the manual communication system.","cur":"asl12"},
{"id":"american-sign-language-3-4","title":"American Sign Language 3-4","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course expands students’ skills in American Sign Language (ASL) and understanding of deaf culture. Students continue to learn comprehension and expression through conversation of the manual communication system.","cur":"asl12"},
{"id":"french-1-2","title":"French 1-2","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is an introduction to the French language and culture. Students learn vocabulary and basic language structures through conversation and written expression.","cur":"french12"},
{"id":"french-1-2-h","title":"French 1-2 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This advanced level course emphasizes the language and culture utilizing techniques from the AP curriculum. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities involving conversation and written expression as preparation for the 3-4 and 5-6 Honors, as well as the AP French program in the 10 th through 12 th grades.","cur":"french12"},
{"id":"french-1-2-ib-myp-honors-gifted","title":"French 1-2 IB MYP Honors/Gifted","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced level course emphasizes rigorous academic activities involving French language conversation and written expression as preparation for the IB Diploma Programme in the 11th and 12th grades.","cur":"french12"},
{"id":"french-3-4","title":"French 3-4","dept":"World Languages","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"French 1-2 or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course expands and refines vocabulary, language structure, and knowledge of French cultures. Students should expect to communicate in conversation and written expression."},
{"id":"french-3-4-h","title":"French 3-4 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"French 1-2 or Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced level course emphasizes the language and culture utilizing techniques from the AP curriculum. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities involving conversation and written expression as preparation for the 5-6 Honors, as well as the AP French program in the 11 th through 12 th grades."},
{"id":"french-5-6-h","title":"French 5-6 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"French 3-4","desc":"This course provides students with daily opportunities to develop all language skills (listening, speaking, reading, and writing) in a rigorous, immersion-based learning environment. The students participate in a wide range of activities, all designed to cultivate deep learning and acquisition of French. All materials used are designed to provide a seamless transition to the AP French Language course the following year"},
{"id":"french-7-8-h","title":"French 7-8 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"French 5-6 H or Placement Criteria","desc":"Communication will be emphasized through compositions, oral presentations, and discussions of current topics, literature, history, and culture."},
{"id":"ap-french-language-and-culture","title":"AP French Language and Culture","dept":"World Languages","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"French 5-6 or Placement Criteria","desc":"Communication will be emphasized through compositions, oral presentations, and discussions of current topics, literature, history, and culture. This course builds on the literary works, writing and speaking emphasized in the Honors 5-6 course. The second semester will concentrate on preparing for the Advanced Placement exam. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam."},
{"id":"mandarin-1-2-h","title":"Mandarin 1-2 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is an introduction to the Mandarin Chinese language and culture. Students will learn vocabulary and basic language structures through conversation and written expression.","cur":"mandarin12"},
{"id":"mandarin-3-4-h","title":"Mandarin 3-4 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Mandarin Chinese 1-2","desc":"This course expands and refines vocabulary, language structure, and knowledge of Chinese cultures. Students should expect to communicate in conversation and written expression."},
{"id":"mandarin-5-6-h","title":"Mandarin 5-6 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Mandarin Chinese 3-4 H/Mandarin Immersion 3-4 H","desc":"This course provides students with daily opportunities to develop all language skills (listening, speaking, reading, and writing) in a rigorous, immersion-based learning environment. Students will participate in a wide range of activities designed to cultivate deep learning and proficiency in Mandarin."},
{"id":"mandarin-7-8-h","title":"Mandarin 7-8 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Mandarin 5-6 H","desc":"This course emphasizes communication by applying interpersonal, interpretive, and presentational skills in real-life situations. It engages students in an exploration of culture in both contemporary and historical contexts. Curriculum will align to AP themes in order to provide a seamless transition to the AP Chinese Language course the following year."},
{"id":"ap-chinese-language-and-culture","title":"AP Chinese Language and Culture","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Mandarin Chinese 7-8 or placement criteria","desc":"This course emphasizes advanced communication by applying interpersonal, interpretive, and presentational skills in real-life situations. It engages students in an exploration of culture in both contemporary and historical contexts. Students will explore AP themes on a 2-year cycle, allowing the course to be taught concurrently with Mandarin Capstone. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam."},
{"id":"mandarin-capstone-h","title":"Mandarin Capstone H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"AP Chinese Language and Culture","desc":"This course emphasizes advanced communication by applying interpersonal, interpretive, and presentational skills in real-life situations. It engages students in an exploration of culture in both contemporary and historical contexts. Students will explore course themes on a 2-year cycle, allowing the course to be taught concurrently with AP Chinese. Additionally, students will complete a capstone project to benefit the larger community. Enrolled students have the option to retake the AP exam if they desire."},
{"id":"spanish-1-2","title":"Spanish 1-2","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is an introduction to the Spanish language and culture. Students learn vocabulary and basic language structures through conversation and written expression.","cur":"spanish12"},
{"id":"spanish-1-2-h","title":"Spanish 1-2 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This advanced level course emphasizes the language and culture utilizing techniques from the AP curriculum. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities involving conversation and written expression as preparation for the 3-4 and 5-6 Honors, as well as the AP Spanish program in the 10 th through 12 th grades. Class requires summer reading.","cur":"spanish12"},
{"id":"spanish-1-2-ib-myp-honors-gifted","title":"Spanish 1-2 IB MYP Honors/Gifted","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This advanced level course emphasizes rigorous academic activities involving Spanish language conversation and written expression as preparation for the IB Diploma Programme in the 11 th & 12 th Grades.","cur":"spanish12"},
{"id":"spanish-3-4","title":"Spanish 3-4","dept":"World Languages","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish 1-2","desc":"This course expands and refines vocabulary, language structure, and knowledge of Spanish cultures. Students should expect to communicate in conversation and written expression.","cur":"spanish34"},
{"id":"spanish-5-6","title":"Spanish 5-6","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish 3-4","desc":"This course develops interpretative, interpersonal, and presentational communication skills in Spanish at the intermediate level. Students will learn vocabulary and intermediate language structures through conversation and written expression, and will increase their understanding of Spanish-speaking cultures.","cur":"spanish56"},
{"id":"spanish-3-4-h","title":"Spanish 3-4 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"SPA 1-2 H or placement criteria","desc":"This advanced level course expands and refines the Spanish language and culture utilizing techniques from the AP curriculum. Students will engage in rigorous academic activities involving conversation and written expression as preparation for the 5-6 Honors and AP Spanish program in the 11 th and 12 th grades. Class requires summer reading.","cur":"spanish34"},
{"id":"spanish-5-6-h","title":"Spanish 5-6 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish 3-4 H or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course provides students with daily opportunities to develop all language skills (listening, speaking, writing, reading, and comprehension) in a rigorous, immersion-based learning environment. The students participate in a wide range of activities, all designed to cultivate deep learning and acquisition of Spanish. All materials used are designed to provide a seamless transition to the AP Spanish Language course the following year.","cur":"spanish56"},
{"id":"spanish-7-8-h","title":"Spanish 7-8 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish 5-6H or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course provides students with daily opportunities to develop all language skills in a rigorous, immersion-based learning environment. Communication will be emphasized through compositions, oral presentations, and discussions of current topics, literature, history, and culture."},
{"id":"spanish-for-spanish-speakers-1-2","title":"Spanish for Spanish Speakers 1-2","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is designed for bilingual Spanish-speaking students, focusing on the development of interpretive reading, presentational writing and presentational speaking skills as outlined in the Arizona World and native Languages Standards.","cur":"spanish12"},
{"id":"spanish-for-spanish-speakers-3-4-h","title":"Spanish for Spanish Speakers 3-4 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish for Spanish Speakers 1-2 or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is designed for bilingual Spanish-speaking students, focusing on the development of interpretive reading, presentational writing and presentational speaking skills as outlined in the Arizona World and Native Languages Standards.","cur":"spanish34"},
{"id":"spanish-for-spanish-speakers-5-6-h","title":"Spanish for Spanish Speakers 5-6 H","dept":"World Languages","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish for Spanish Speakers 3-4H or Placement Criteria","desc":"This course is designed for native Spanish speakers. It will emphasize the study of Spanish language literature, news, music, and scholarly articles on the topics of identity, family, education, work, leisure, gastronomy, health and well-being, community, and environment. The course will increase writing and reading skills and strategies, and will include rigorous studies of the culture and geography of Spanish speaking countries.","cur":"spanish56"},
{"id":"ap-spanish-language-and-culture","title":"AP Spanish Language and Culture","dept":"World Languages","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish 5-6H or Placement Criteria","desc":"Communication will be emphasized through compositions, oral presentations, and discussions of current topics, literature, history, and culture. The second semester will concentrate on preparing for the Advanced Placement exam. Enrolled students are expected to take the AP exam."},
{"id":"ap-spanish-literature-and-culture","title":"AP Spanish Literature and Culture","dept":"World Languages","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Spanish 5-6 or Instructor Approval","desc":"This course builds on the skills acquired in AP Spanish Language and Culture. The course uses a thematic approach to introduce students to representative texts from several geographic regions. Students develop proficiencies across the full range of communication, thereby honing their critical reading and analytical writing skills."},
{"id":"student-aides","title":"Student Aides","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Students may earn 1/2 credit (.5) per semester as a student aide. A maximum of one credit may be recorded on the transcripts. Duties will vary."},
{"id":"advanced-study-skills","title":"Advanced Study Skills","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"The purpose for this course is to prepare students for academic success in the high school arena. This course includes a multitude of study and organizational tools to choose from in order to be successful in classrooms across the disciplines. This class that will provide students with an unique array of tools to help ensure academic success."},
{"id":"advisory","title":"Advisory","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","desc":"Advisory is a period set aside for small groups of students to meet with their faculty advisors. During these sessions, students will receive advisement on grade level specific topics or projects."},
{"id":"ap-business-principles-and-personal-finance","title":"AP Business Principles and Personal Finance","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"Students will learn a powerful combination of college-level business education and essential life skills. Students explore key business disciplines including entrepreneurship, marketing, finance, accounting, and management through real-world projects and case studies while simultaneously mastering personal finance fundamentals such as budgeting, saving, investing, and credit management.","cur":"finmath"},
{"id":"ap-seminar","title":"AP Seminar","dept":"Electives & Other","credit":"1","desc":"AP Seminar is a foundational course for gifted and advanced learners that explores complex real-world topics by analyzing them from multiple perspectives. Using an inquiry-based approach, students will develop crucial research, collaboration, and communication skills to synthesize information from diverse sources. In this course students will craft and deliver compelling, evidence-based arguments in research essays and individual and team formal presentations. GRADE:10-12 LENGTH: Year"},
{"id":"career-development-tsw","title":"Career Development (TSW)","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Students with an IEP or 504 Plan and are in","desc":"This course prepares students for the world of work. Topics will be job skills, employment success, and self-determination in the employment world."},
{"id":"career-inquiry-tsw","title":"Career Inquiry (TSW)","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"10-11","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Students with an IEP or 504 Plan and are in","desc":"This course prepares students for the world of work. Topics will be job seeking skills, career exploration, and on-the-job skills."},
{"id":"change-the-world-gifted-capstone","title":"Change the World Gifted Capstone","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"Gifted Capstone a year-long course that has students investigate real-world issues from multiple perspectives. Students learn to synthesize information from different sources, develop their own lines of reasoning in research-based written essays, and design and deliver oral and visual presentations, both individually and as part of a team."},
{"id":"gaming-concepts-1","title":"Gaming Concepts 1","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Gaming Concepts 1 engages students in exploration of gaming history, game genres, technology, troubleshooting, and college and career readiness with a technology focus. The course allows students to practice good gamer health and engage in digital citizenship, teamwork, and reflection."},
{"id":"gaming-concepts-2","title":"Gaming Concepts 2","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"Gaming Concepts 2 allows students to continue learning about gaming technology. They will build college and career readiness skills such as communication, teamwork, critical thinking, and problem solving. They will also explore technology-related careers and learn skills they will need beyond high school."},
{"id":"gifted-seminar-h","title":"Gifted Seminar H","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"CogAT scores in 90+ percentile in any category","desc":"This course is designed to provide a series of opportunities for gifted learners to understand how the gifted mind works, explore areas of interest, develop selected skills, and reflect on the learning process. These opportunities allow academic pursuit in greater depth, in different formats, and/or across interdisciplinary lines. The course covers a series of experiences including, but not limited to individual or small group projects, whole-class studies, Socratic discussion, guest speaker presentations, field trips, development of leadership skills, etc."},
{"id":"work-experience","title":"Work Experience","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is supervised work experience with a minimum of 5 hours per week in a paid position. Students will meet for seat time until released to their internship sites. Students must provide their own transportation to their work sites and be 16 years of age or older."},
{"id":"preventing-substance-abuse-across-the-lifespan","title":"Preventing Substance Abuse across the Lifespan","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course examines the various types of substance abuse and addresses the adverse impact of using or abusing drugs. Substance development from manufacturing through methods of delivery will be presented, as well as the biological mechanisms involved once ingested. Aspects of substance abuse will also be presented to include types of addictions and relapse prevention models used for treating abuse.","cur":"dv:HEALTH"},
{"id":"transition-exploration","title":"Transition Exploration","dept":"Electives & Other","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"IEP or 504 Placement","desc":"This course will provide foundations for high school success. Topics will include but are not limited to: high school success strategies such as note taking, organization, study skills, time management, self-awareness, active engagement, healthy lifestyles, selfdetermination, career exploration and conflict resolution."},
{"id":"transition-skills-field-experience","title":"Transition Skills Field Experience","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"11-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","prereq":"IEP or 504 Placement","desc":"This course prepares students for the work force. Students will work five (5) hours per week in on campus or community environments."},
{"id":"transition-skills","title":"Transition Skills","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"IEP or 504 Placement","desc":"This course prepares students for the world of work. Topics will be job seeking skills, career exploration, and on-the-job skills."},
{"id":"learning-center-support","title":"Learning Center Support","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"IEP","desc":"This course is designed to offer supplemental support to students with disabilities who have classes in the general education environment."},
{"id":"peer-facilitator","title":"Peer-Facilitator","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"10-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Interview","desc":"This course is for students who desire to help fellow classmates achieve success in high school. Activities include: academic tutoring, peer mediation, and participation in group activities and discussions. Students will receive a grade of pass/fail. A select team of students are trained to reach out to students who need conflict resolution, “TLC” and support. They learn to be good listeners without giving advice and how to make referrals for professional help when needed."},
{"id":"student-government","title":"Student Government","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Elected to Office","desc":"This class consists of those students who are elected to student body and class offices. Classroom activities are designed to teach leadership skills, along with student government functions. Students will be placed in this course following student body elections."},
{"id":"academic-decathlon-h","title":"Academic Decathlon H","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","prereq":"Placement Criteria","desc":"Students will study the ten areas of the Academic Decathlon: Literature, math, science, economics, social studies, fine arts, composition, speech, interview and super quiz. This humanities-oriented class will prepare students to compete in the Academic Decathlon. Some after-school involvement may be necessary."},
{"id":"academic-lab","title":"Academic Lab","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester/Year","credit":".5","prereq":"Counselor Approval","desc":"This course is designed to assist students who are experiencing academic difficulties and who could benefit from study skills instruction, peer tutoring and group activities and discussion."},
{"id":"computer-technology-i","title":"Computer Technology I","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course will explore emerging computer-based technology and its uses. It will help students become proficient with today’s technology and model skills that will help them be better 21 st century learners and citizens. Topics will include computer hardware and software, networks, use of technology in business and society, word processing, spreadsheets, databases, presentation software, security and privacy issues, and digital citizenship.","cur":"comptech"},
{"id":"computer-technology-ii","title":"Computer Technology II","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"9-12","length":"Semester","credit":".5","desc":"This course will allow students to work in a project-based environment to explore 21 st century digital tools that have the power to engage and enhance communication and collaboration in college and careers. Students will work with online tools, browser functions, resource discovery systems, coding, and file transfers. Students will be introduced to HTML, XHTML, & CSS. Students will plan, design, and publish a web site.","cur":"comptech"},
{"id":"technology-facilitator","title":"Technology Facilitator","dept":"Electives & Other","grade":"11-12","length":"Year","credit":"1","desc":"This course is for students who desire to learn more about technology implementation and integration. Students will develop skills in technology trouble-shooting and training. Activities include: trouble-shooting computer issues, device inventory management, new student and staff orientation, app and web 2.0 research for classroom use, and hardware maintenance. Students will receive a pass/fail grade."}];
